According to the Express.js guide, you define error-handling middleware functions in the same way as other middleware functions, except they require four arguments instead of three: `(err, req, res, next)` [1]. 

Here is an example of how to write an error-handling middleware function:

```javascript
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err.stack);
  res.status(500).send('Something broke!');
});
```
*(Or using TypeScript definitions)* [1]:
```typescript
import { type Request, type Response, type NextFunction } from 'express';
app.use((err: Error, req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
  console.error(err.stack);
  res.status(500).send('Something broke!');
});
```

You must define error-handling middleware last, after all other `app.use()` calls and routes [2].
